1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
This video turns a grammar into code. The technique is recursive descent.

2
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:18,413
We learn four rules that map each part of a grammar to code. Then we write and run recognizers for four question bank questions. At the end we turn a recognizer into an evaluator.

3
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:27,503
In this course the input is always a string. A recognizer answers one question: is the string in the language? Yes or no.

4
00:00:27,953 --> 00:00:31,003
A parser also builds a tree, the A S T.

5
00:00:31,453 --> 00:00:34,760
A compiler outputs an executable program.

6
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:40,031
An interpreter, or evaluator, computes a value, such as an integer.

7
00:00:40,481 --> 00:00:55,073
All four follow the same pattern. In the labs, a recognizer method returns void. The lexer throws an exception when the input is wrong. So an exception means reject, and normal completion means accept.

8
00:00:55,523 --> 00:01:06,254
Recursive descent is a way to write a parser by hand, from a grammar. Four rules do it. Rule 1. Each nonterminal becomes a method with the same name.

9
00:01:06,704 --> 00:01:17,541
Rule 2. Each terminal becomes a call to consume on the lexer. Consume takes the next token if it is the right one, and throws an exception if it is not.

10
00:01:17,991 --> 00:01:22,343
Rule 3. Each repetition, star or plus, becomes a loop.

11
00:01:22,793 --> 00:01:30,985
Rule 4. Each alternation, the bar, becomes an if statement. The if looks at the next token to choose the alternative.

12
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:42,529
A grammar is a mathematical specification for a language. It is also a specification for the parser. With these rules, you derive the code from the grammar.

13
00:01:42,979 --> 00:01:51,064
The recognizer reads tokens from a lexer. The lexer has a position in the token stream. Here the next token is metric.

14
00:01:51,514 --> 00:02:00,474
inspect looks at the next token and returns true or false. It does not move the position. Use inspect to choose an alternative.

15
00:02:00,924 --> 00:02:09,244
consume takes the next token, if it is the expected one, and moves the position forward. Use consume for every terminal.

16
00:02:09,694 --> 00:02:20,190
If the next token is not the expected one, consume throws an exception. Here consume dot throws, because the next token is 3. That is a reject.

17
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:28,128
There are also forms for the end of input and for token classes, such as consume I D and consume Int.

18
00:02:28,578 --> 00:02:34,295
On an exam, you can assume a lexer like the one in the labs. The answers are pseudo-code.

19
00:02:34,745 --> 00:02:41,807
Question 3.1.1. A list of names. List goes to Name star. Write a recognizer.

20
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:45,777
Name goes to I D, a name token from the lexer.

21
00:02:46,227 --> 00:02:47,869
Pause the video and try it.

22
00:02:48,319 --> 00:02:53,354
Apply the rules. List is a nonterminal, so it becomes a method, List.

23
00:02:53,804 --> 00:03:08,161
The right side of List is a repetition, Name star. So List has a while loop. Inside the loop is a call to Name, because the rule uses Name. The loop runs while the next token is not the end of input.

24
00:03:08,611 --> 00:03:15,737
List is the start symbol. After the loop, consume E O F. This checks that the whole input was used.

25
00:03:16,187 --> 00:03:25,040
Name is a nonterminal, so it becomes a method too. Its right side is the terminal I D, so Name calls consume I D.

26
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:29,906
Run the recognizer on two names, Ana and Bo. List starts.

27
00:03:30,356 --> 00:03:35,988
inspect E O F returns false. There is input left, so the loop body runs.

28
00:03:36,438 --> 00:03:37,803
List calls Name.

29
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:43,075
Name consumes I D: the token Ana. The position moves to Bo.

30
00:03:43,525 --> 00:03:47,130
Back in the loop. inspect E O F is false again.

31
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:49,393
So List calls Name again.

32
00:03:49,843 --> 00:03:51,337
Name consumes Bo.

33
00:03:51,787 --> 00:03:55,733
Now inspect E O F returns true, and the loop stops.

34
00:03:56,183 --> 00:03:58,295
consume E O F succeeds.

35
00:03:58,745 --> 00:04:02,927
List returns normally, so the recognizer accepts the input.

36
00:04:03,377 --> 00:04:10,395
Now the input Ana, 7. The first name is consumed as before. inspect E O F is false.

37
00:04:10,845 --> 00:04:12,403
So List calls Name.

38
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:18,271
Name calls consume I D, but the next token is the number 7, not a name.

39
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:23,137
consume throws an exception. The recognizer rejects the input.

40
00:04:23,587 --> 00:04:31,971
Question 3.1.2 uses the measurement grammar from question 1.1.1. Write a recognizer.

41
00:04:32,421 --> 00:04:35,173
Digits is a token class from the lexer.

42
00:04:35,623 --> 00:04:37,266
Pause the video and try it.

43
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:41,727
Measurement is the start nonterminal. It becomes a method.

44
00:04:42,177 --> 00:04:54,422
Its right side has a bar, so the method has an if statement. Each alternative starts with a different token: metric or imperial. So one inspect chooses the alternative.

45
00:04:54,872 --> 00:04:59,544
If the next token is metric, consume metric and call DecimalNumber.

46
00:04:59,994 --> 00:05:04,666
Else if it is imperial, consume imperial and call FractionalNumber.

47
00:05:05,116 --> 00:05:09,788
Else, no alternative fits, so throw an exception. That is a reject.

48
00:05:10,238 --> 00:05:15,081
Measurement is the start symbol. So at the end it calls consume E O F.

49
00:05:15,531 --> 00:05:22,251
DecimalNumber has no bar. It is a sequence: consume Digits, consume dot, consume Digits.

50
00:05:22,701 --> 00:05:27,671
FractionalNumber is also a sequence: Digits, Digits, slash, Digits.

51
00:05:28,121 --> 00:05:34,756
The posted solution misspells FractionalNumber in one place. The method name must match the call.

52
00:05:35,206 --> 00:05:40,945
Run it on metric 3.875. Four tokens. Measurement starts.

53
00:05:41,395 --> 00:05:46,643
Measurement inspects the next token. It is metric, so the first branch runs.

54
00:05:47,093 --> 00:05:50,293
consume metric. The position moves to 3.

55
00:05:50,743 --> 00:05:52,279
Call DecimalNumber.

56
00:05:52,729 --> 00:05:54,819
consume Digits takes 3.

57
00:05:55,269 --> 00:05:57,317
consume dot takes the dot.

58
00:05:57,767 --> 00:06:00,967
consume Digits takes 875.

59
00:06:01,417 --> 00:06:03,103
DecimalNumber returns.

60
00:06:03,553 --> 00:06:06,603
Back in Measurement, consume E O F succeeds.

61
00:06:07,053 --> 00:06:09,635
Measurement returns normally: accept.

62
00:06:10,085 --> 00:06:14,181
Now imperial 3 slash 8. inspect metric is false.

63
00:06:14,631 --> 00:06:16,508
inspect imperial is true.

64
00:06:16,958 --> 00:06:18,537
consume imperial.

65
00:06:18,987 --> 00:06:20,587
Call FractionalNumber.

66
00:06:21,037 --> 00:06:23,127
consume Digits takes 3.

67
00:06:23,577 --> 00:06:27,887
The grammar needs a second Digits, but the next token is the slash.

68
00:06:28,337 --> 00:06:35,974
consume throws: reject. This grammar needs a whole number before the fraction, such as 3 7 slash 8.

69
00:06:36,424 --> 00:06:44,957
Question 3.1.3 uses the professions grammar from question 1.1.2. Write a recognizer.

70
00:06:45,407 --> 00:06:47,050
Pause the video and try it.

71
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:55,799
ProfessionalSkills has three alternatives, so it has an if with three branches. Each alternative is a nonterminal.

72
00:06:56,249 --> 00:07:06,723
To choose, look at the first word. Engineer or Architect means Technical. Physician or Surgeon means Medical. Lawyer or Judge means Legal.

73
00:07:07,173 --> 00:07:09,435
If no first word fits, throw.

74
00:07:09,885 --> 00:07:15,026
ProfessionalSkills is the start symbol, so it ends with consume E O F.

75
00:07:15,476 --> 00:07:22,644
Technical has a group with a bar inside, so it has its own if. Engineer is followed by calculates.

76
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:25,334
Architect is followed by draws.

77
00:07:25,784 --> 00:07:30,712
After the group, consume design. Medical and Legal have the same shape.

78
00:07:31,162 --> 00:07:46,885
The posted solution misspells calculates in the consume call. With that typo, the correct sentence Engineer calculates design is rejected. A python copy of our code accepts exactly the six sentences of the grammar.

79
00:07:47,335 --> 00:07:53,479
Run it on Engineer cuts patient. inspect Engineer is true, so the first branch runs.

80
00:07:53,929 --> 00:07:55,209
Call Technical.

81
00:07:55,659 --> 00:07:58,816
Technical inspects Engineer again. It is true.

82
00:07:59,266 --> 00:08:01,122
Technical consumes Engineer.

83
00:08:01,572 --> 00:08:05,604
Now Technical needs calculates. The next token is cuts.

84
00:08:06,054 --> 00:08:14,417
consume throws, and the recognizer rejects the sentence. This matches the stuck derivation in question 1.1.2.

85
00:08:14,867 --> 00:08:24,061
Why does one inspect call choose the right branch? Each alternative of Measurement begins with a different token: metric or imperial.

86
00:08:24,511 --> 00:08:29,098
So one token of lookahead decides. Anything else is a reject.

87
00:08:29,548 --> 00:08:45,079
Grammars where one token of lookahead always decides are L L one. Left to right, leftmost derivation, one token of lookahead. Recursive descent works directly for them. Video 19 shows the full test.

88
00:08:45,529 --> 00:08:54,809
A recursive descent recognizer always works on the leftmost unfinished part first. So its calls follow the leftmost derivation.

89
00:08:55,259 --> 00:09:08,869
Question 3.2.1. An L L one calculator grammar. It is a left-factored form of the grammar in question 1.2.1. Write a recognizer, then an evaluator.

90
00:09:09,319 --> 00:09:13,373
Note the epsilon alternatives in TermTail and FactorTail.

91
00:09:13,823 --> 00:09:15,465
Pause the video and try it.

92
00:09:15,915 --> 00:09:22,806
First, the new part: an epsilon alternative. TermTail goes to plus Expr, or to epsilon.

93
00:09:23,256 --> 00:09:29,507
If the next token is a plus, take the first alternative: consume the plus and call Expr.

94
00:09:29,957 --> 00:09:39,599
Otherwise take epsilon. Epsilon consumes nothing, so the method does nothing and returns. In code, that is an if with no else.

95
00:09:40,049 --> 00:09:43,015
The caller then goes on with the same next token.

96
00:09:43,465 --> 00:09:55,433
S, Expr and Term have one alternative each. Each is a sequence of nonterminals, so each method is a sequence of calls. Expr calls Term, then TermTail.

97
00:09:55,883 --> 00:10:10,368
Factor has two alternatives. An open parenthesis starts the second one. So: if the next token is an open parenthesis, consume it, call Expr, and consume the close parenthesis. Else consume an Int.

98
00:10:10,818 --> 00:10:15,511
TermTail and FactorTail are the epsilon pattern: an if with no else.

99
00:10:15,961 --> 00:10:37,252
The posted S method calls only Expr. It does not check the end of input. Then the input 1 plus 2, close parenthesis, is accepted, which is wrong. Add consume E O F, as in the earlier questions. A python check compared our recognizer with the grammar on all strings up to 7 tokens.

100
00:10:37,702 --> 00:10:44,614
Run the recognizer on 1 plus 2. S calls Expr, Expr calls Term, and Term calls Factor.

101
00:10:45,064 --> 00:10:48,925
Factor inspects for an open parenthesis. There is none.

102
00:10:49,375 --> 00:10:51,722
So Factor consumes the Int, 1.

103
00:10:52,172 --> 00:10:56,652
Term calls FactorTail. The next token is a plus, not a times.

104
00:10:57,102 --> 00:11:00,067
So FactorTail takes epsilon and returns.

105
00:11:00,517 --> 00:11:04,059
Expr calls TermTail. The next token is a plus.

106
00:11:04,509 --> 00:11:06,407
TermTail consumes the plus.

107
00:11:06,857 --> 00:11:08,649
TermTail calls Expr.

108
00:11:09,099 --> 00:11:13,195
The new Expr calls Term, then Factor. Factor consumes 2.

109
00:11:13,645 --> 00:11:17,443
FactorTail sees the end of input. It takes epsilon.

110
00:11:17,893 --> 00:11:22,074
TermTail also sees the end of input, and takes epsilon.

111
00:11:22,524 --> 00:11:27,324
All calls return. S calls consume E O F. It succeeds.

112
00:11:27,774 --> 00:11:30,142
S returns normally: accept.

113
00:11:30,592 --> 00:11:37,376
Draw every call of that run as a tree. Each call is a node, and its children are the calls it makes.

114
00:11:37,826 --> 00:11:42,818
Each token that a call consumes is a leaf below it: 1, plus, and 2.

115
00:11:43,268 --> 00:11:46,980
A method that took epsilon has no tokens below it.

116
00:11:47,430 --> 00:12:02,705
This tree has the same shape as the parse tree of 1 plus 2 in the grammar. A python check compared the two trees. So a recognizer builds the parse tree in its calls, even if it never stores it. A parser stores it.

117
00:12:03,155 --> 00:12:18,813
Now the evaluator. Start from the recognizer and make two changes. Each method returns an int, and each method does the computation. Expr returns Term plus TermTail. Term returns Factor times FactorTail.

118
00:12:19,263 --> 00:12:24,319
Factor returns the value inside the parentheses, or the Int it consumes.

119
00:12:24,769 --> 00:12:36,503
TermTail returns the value of the Expr after the plus. For epsilon, it must return something that does not change the sum. That is 0, the identity for plus.

120
00:12:36,953 --> 00:12:41,454
FactorTail returns 1 for epsilon, the identity for times.

121
00:12:41,904 --> 00:12:46,704
The evaluator computes while it parses. It does not build an A S T.

122
00:12:47,154 --> 00:12:53,874
Test the evaluator on 2 times 3 plus 4. The answer should be 10. Factor consumes 2.

123
00:12:54,324 --> 00:12:55,881
Factor returns 2.

124
00:12:56,331 --> 00:12:58,187
FactorTail sees a times.

125
00:12:58,637 --> 00:13:00,408
It consumes the times.

126
00:13:00,858 --> 00:13:06,917
Then FactorTail calls Expr, as the grammar says: FactorTail goes to times Expr.

127
00:13:07,367 --> 00:13:10,247
Inside that Expr, Factor returns 3.

128
00:13:10,697 --> 00:13:17,523
TermTail sees the plus. The finished Term call now shows as one row: its tokens and its value.

129
00:13:17,973 --> 00:13:19,531
It consumes the plus.

130
00:13:19,981 --> 00:13:21,666
It calls Expr again.

131
00:13:22,116 --> 00:13:23,759
Factor returns 4.

132
00:13:24,209 --> 00:13:27,857
FactorTail sees the end of input. It returns 1.

133
00:13:28,307 --> 00:13:31,912
TermTail also sees the end. It returns 0.

134
00:13:32,362 --> 00:13:37,823
Now the values come back up. The inner Expr returns 3 plus 4, which is 7.

135
00:13:38,273 --> 00:13:40,172
FactorTail returns 7.

136
00:13:40,622 --> 00:13:44,206
So Term returns 2 times 7, which is 14.

137
00:13:44,656 --> 00:13:47,728
The evaluator returns 14, not 10.

138
00:13:48,178 --> 00:14:02,407
The cause is in the grammar of the question. FactorTail goes to times Expr. So after a times, the whole rest of the input, 3 plus 4, becomes one Expr. The evaluator computes 2 times 7.

139
00:14:02,857 --> 00:14:23,807
Our fix: FactorTail goes to times Term. Then the times takes only the next Term, 3. The value is 2 times 3, plus 4, which is 10. In the code, FactorTail returns Term instead of Expr. Question 1.2.1 has the same idea: Term goes to Factor times Term.

140
00:14:24,257 --> 00:14:42,561
Both grammars accept the same strings. They are weakly equivalent, from video 16. So the recognizer is correct either way. Only the trees differ, and the evaluator follows the tree. A python check ran both versions on all strings up to 7 tokens.

141
00:14:43,011 --> 00:14:58,093
The notes have a job interview question. Evaluate exponents, with integers and parentheses. Exponentiation is right associative: 2 to the 3 to the 2 is 2 to the 9, which is 512.

142
00:14:58,543 --> 00:15:04,922
The grammar is right recursive and left factored. Tail goes to caret Number, or epsilon.

143
00:15:05,372 --> 00:15:12,177
The code follows the same rules. Number calls Base, then Tail, and returns base to the power exp.

144
00:15:12,627 --> 00:15:19,667
Tail consumes a caret and returns Number. For epsilon it returns 1, because x to the 1 is x.

145
00:15:20,117 --> 00:15:30,528
Base returns the value in parentheses, or the integer. This code uses an array and a position instead of a lexer, but the technique is the same.

146
00:15:30,978 --> 00:15:37,549
Run it on 2 caret 3 caret 2. The caret is the exponent sign. Base consumes 2.

147
00:15:37,999 --> 00:15:39,492
Base returns 2.

148
00:15:39,942 --> 00:15:41,435
Tail sees a caret.

149
00:15:41,885 --> 00:15:43,635
It consumes the caret.

150
00:15:44,085 --> 00:15:45,514
Tail calls Number.

151
00:15:45,964 --> 00:15:47,991
The second Number gets base 3.

152
00:15:48,441 --> 00:15:51,406
Its Tail sees another caret and consumes it.

153
00:15:51,856 --> 00:15:53,819
The last Number gets base 2.

154
00:15:54,269 --> 00:15:57,618
Its Tail sees the end of input and returns 1.

155
00:15:58,068 --> 00:16:01,204
So that Number returns 2 to the 1, which is 2.

156
00:16:01,654 --> 00:16:05,110
The middle Number returns 3 to the 2, which is 9.

157
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:09,997
The first Number returns 2 to the 9, which is 512.

158
00:16:10,447 --> 00:16:16,869
S returns 512. The right recursion makes the operator right associative.

159
00:16:17,319 --> 00:16:24,167
The notes list common mistakes for these questions. First: the grammar does not accept a single integer.

160
00:16:24,617 --> 00:16:31,550
Second: the associativity is wrong, or the grammar has common prefixes, so it is not L L one.

161
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Third: an evaluator builds an explicit A S T. That makes the code larger. Compute the value while you parse.

162
00:16:40,450 --> 00:16:45,677
From the posted solutions in this video: check the end of input in the start method.

163
00:16:46,127 --> 00:16:49,455
And spell every terminal exactly as in the grammar.

164
00:16:49,905 --> 00:16:56,433
In an evaluator, an epsilon alternative returns the identity element of its operator.

165
00:16:56,883 --> 00:17:07,123
To summarize. A nonterminal becomes a method. A terminal becomes consume. A repetition becomes a loop. An alternation becomes an if.

166
00:17:07,573 --> 00:17:17,663
inspect looks at the next token without moving. consume takes it, or throws. An exception means reject, and normal completion means accept.

167
00:17:18,113 --> 00:17:25,281
An epsilon alternative becomes an if with no else. The start method ends with consume E O F.

168
00:17:25,731 --> 00:17:31,086
The calls follow the leftmost derivation, so the call tree is the parse tree.

169
00:17:31,536 --> 00:17:43,973
For an evaluator, each method returns a value, and an epsilon alternative returns the identity element. Next video: ambiguity, precedence and associativity.
